-- Passage rg12-main-idea-summary-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('466576c1-bad1-576d-8384-9b3c0b9d2894', 'rg12-main-idea-summary-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Chair That Changed the Game$q$, $q$Early wheelchair basketball looked very different from the fast game played today. In its first decades, athletes used heavy everyday chairs designed for hospital hallways, not for sprinting, turning and colliding. The chairs had tall backs, large handles and wheels set straight up and down. Players could push forward well enough, but sharp turns were slow and a hard bump could tip a chair over.

Change came largely from the athletes themselves. In the fictional city of Larkmoor, a player named Darius Okafor began working with a local welder in the evenings, asking for small changes after every practice. They lowered the seat to give him more stability, removed the handles he never used, and replaced the steel frame with lighter tubing. Teammates noticed how quickly he moved and asked for chairs of their own.

The most important change was the tilt of the wheels. By angling the tops of the wheels inward, a design called camber, the builders gave the chair a wider base at the floor. That wider base made it far harder to tip and allowed tighter, quicker turns. A small extra wheel at the back kept players from falling backward when they leaned to shoot or reach for a rebound.

These improvements did more than make players comfortable. They changed how the sport was played. Lighter, more stable chairs allowed faster breaks down the court, tighter defense and plays that had once been impossible. Coaches developed new strategies, and the game became more exciting to watch, which helped attract new players and fans.

Today, sports chairs are often fitted to each athlete's body, much as a runner's shoes are chosen for a particular foot. Manufacturers still invite players to test new designs and suggest changes. The history of the basketball chair shows that good equipment often comes from close cooperation between the people who build it and the people who use it, and that better tools can reshape the very activity they were made for.$q$, 'draft')
